# What a gateway is, carried across a firmware upgrade.
#
# sysupgrade keeps /etc/config/* and whatever is listed in keep.d, and nothing
# else. Before this file existed that meant /etc/config/pulzr survived — the
# UCI that says "adopted" — while everything that makes the adoption usable was
# thrown away. An upgraded gateway believed it was adopted and could not prove
# who it was: the claim secret was gone, so its reports were refused.
#
# The WireGuard keypair is kept for the same reason: the hub knows this
# gateway by its public key, so a new key after every upgrade would mean
# re-enrolling, and a tunnel that is down until it does.
#
# board.conf is deliberately NOT here. It describes the image, and has to come
# from the new one or the gateway would report the old version forever.
/etc/pulzr/claim/
/etc/parkeze/claim/
/etc/pulzr/console/
/etc/parkeze/console/
/etc/pulzr/wireguard/
/etc/parkeze/wireguard/
/etc/pulzr/recovery.pub
/etc/parkeze/recovery.pub
# The upgrade journal, which exists to be read on the boot *after* an upgrade:
# what we came from, who asked, and which stashed image undoes it. Written by
# the old system and read by the new one, so the one reboot it has to survive
# is the one it is about. In /tmp or /var/run it would be gone exactly when it
# was wanted. See pulzr-firmware.
/etc/pulzr/upgrade/
/etc/parkeze/upgrade/
# Keys a technician provisioned on this unit. Pulzr's own key is NOT here:
# it comes from the image, so that rotating it is a firmware upgrade rather
# than a visit. See pulzr-fleet-keys.
/etc/pulzr/site-keys
/etc/parkeze/site-keys
# Speed test history (pulzr-speedtest), so its chart does not start over at
# every upgrade.
/etc/pulzr/speedtest/
/etc/parkeze/speedtest/
# The add-on journal: who installed or removed what, and why one was undone
# (pulzr-addon). What the gateway wants installed is UCI, in
# /etc/config/pulzr_addons, which sysupgrade keeps anyway; the add-ons'
# own settings are in keep.d/pulzr-addons, generated with the image. Not
# mirrored under /etc/parkeze: no ParkezeOS image has add-ons.
/etc/pulzr/addons/
# SSH host keys. Pulzr Cloud pins the one a gateway reports, so a new key after
# an upgrade is a gateway nobody can open a shell on until it reports again.
# dropbear's as well as sshd's: they are the same keys (see pulzr-ssh-setup),
# and a rollback to a dropbear image should present the one that is pinned.
/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key
/etc/ssh/ssh_host_ed25519_key.pub
/etc/ssh/ssh_host_rsa_key
/etc/ssh/ssh_host_rsa_key.pub
/etc/dropbear/dropbear_ed25519_host_key
/etc/dropbear/dropbear_rsa_host_key
# ── ParkezeOS compatibility ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every /etc/pulzr path above is listed under its old /etc/parkeze name as
# well. pulzr-legacy keeps the two in step (it writes the old names just
# before each sysupgrade), so rolling back to a ParkezeOS image finds the
# claim identity, the WireGuard key and everything else where that image
# looks for it. The marker says which side is current; see pulzr-legacy.
/etc/parkeze/.synced-from-pulzr
